---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Project console">
  <h1>Project console</h1>
  <p>
    The console is a page the server serves at <code>/console/</code> where
    the people who own a project manage it with their own account. They do
    not need the operator's admin secret, which reaches every project on the
    server. A project's settings decide who can sign in to it, so this is
    where its owner chooses whether it has open sign-up, tenants, individual
    accounts, child accounts and so on.
  </p>

  <h2 id="turning-it-on">Turning it on</h2>
  <p>The console is off by default and needs the postgres driver.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Console settings">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Variable</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>GATEWAY_CONSOLE_ENABLED</code></td>
        <td><code>false</code></td>
        <td>Serves the page and its RPCs.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>GATEWAY_CONSOLE_SELF_SERVICE_PROJECTS_ENABLED</code></td>
        <td><code>false</code></td>
        <td>Lets a console account create a project it owns. Needs the console.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>GATEWAY_CONSOLE_MAX_PROJECTS_PER_ACCOUNT</code></td>
        <td><code>10</code></td>
        <td>How many projects one account may own, up to 100.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Creating a project also spends a per-address budget the size of
    <code>GATEWAY_RATE_LIMIT_SIGNUP_PER_IP</code>, so one address cannot make
    accounts to get around the per-account limit.
  </p>

  <h2 id="accounts">Who signs in</h2>
  <p>
    A console account is an ordinary account of the server's own (default)
    project. It signs in the way any account of that project does, so
    lockout, two-step verification and session revocation all apply, and
    <code>GATEWAY_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ACCESS_MODE</code> decides who can have
    one. With that mode <code>open</code> and self-service projects on,
    anyone can create a project.
  </p>
  <p>
    The console admits an active person whose email is verified. An agent, an
    anonymous account and an account a tenant manages are refused, and so is
    a token of any other project: a customer of a project is not one of its
    administrators.
  </p>

  <h2 id="projects">Projects and members</h2>
  <p>
    A project has members, each an <code>owner</code> or an <code>admin</code>;
    both may change its settings. The account that creates a project owns it.
    A project the caller is not a member of is refused exactly as one that
    does not exist.
  </p>
  <p>
    A new project comes with one publishable key, which an application sends
    in the <code>X-Project-Key</code> header to select it, and with
    <strong>no access mode: nobody can sign up or sign in until its owner
    chooses one.</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    A project the operator created with <code>AdminCreateProject</code> has
    no members and appears in nobody's console. Until there is a call for
    it, the operator adds the row (<code>user_id</code> is an account of the
    default project):
  </p>
  <Code lang="sql" code={`INSERT INTO project_members (project_id, user_id, role, created_at_ms)
VALUES ('<project id>', '<account id>', 'owner', (extract(epoch FROM now()) * 1000)::bigint);`} />

  <h2 id="settings">What a member can change</h2>
  <p>
    Members read and write an allow-listed part of the project's
    <code>config_json</code>. Everything else stays the operator's and is
    neither shown to a member nor writable by one.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Member-editable settings">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Members</th><th scope="col">Operator only</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>access</code>, <code>anonymous</code>, <code>login</code>,
          <code>recovery_emails</code>, <code>tenants</code>,
          <code>guardian</code>, <code>age_gate</code>,
          <code>jurisdictions</code>, <code>cors</code>,
          <code>accounts.email_signup</code>,
          <code>accounts.username_signup</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>oauth</code>, <code>accounts.domain</code>,
          <code>branding</code>, <code>email_delivery</code>,
          <code>phone</code>, <code>passkey</code>, <code>assurance</code>,
          <code>products</code>
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    While any product of the project sets a <code>minimum_age_band</code>,
    <code>age_gate</code> and <code>jurisdictions</code> are the operator's
    as well: the guardrail is built on them, and a member who lowered a
    threshold would let every account through it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Settings are shown as the server reads them: under their canonical
    names, with a setting at its default left out. A member's change passes
    the same validation as an operator's
    <code>UpsertProjectConfig</code>. It governs sign-ins once the server's
    project cache expires
    (<code>GATEWAY_PROJECT_RESOLUTION_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS</code>, 30 by
    default), as an operator's change does.
  </p>

  <h2 id="api">Calling it from code</h2>
  <p>
    The page uses four RPCs, and a script can call them the same way with the
    account's access token. The request must resolve to the default project,
    so it carries no <code>X-Project-Key</code>.
  </p>
  <Code lang="bash" code={`# Sign in as a console account.
TOKEN=$(curl -s https://auth.example.com/identity.v1.IdentityService/PasswordLogin \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"email":"owner@example.com","password":"..."}' | jq -r .accessToken)

# Create a project, then open it to sign-up.
curl -s https://auth.example.com/identity.v1.IdentityService/CreateProject \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"name":"Acme Storefront"}'

curl -s https://auth.example.com/identity.v1.IdentityService/UpdateProjectSettings \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"projectId":"<project id>","settingsJson":"{\\"access\\":{\\"mode\\":\\"open\\"}}","expectedVersion":"0"}'`} />
  <p>
    <code>UpdateProjectSettings</code> replaces each section it names and
    leaves the rest alone. It takes the <code>version</code> the caller last
    read from <code>GetProjectSettings</code> and answers
    <code>ABORTED</code> when the project's config has changed since, so two
    people editing at once cannot overwrite each other.
  </p>

  <h2 id="audit">Audit</h2>
  <p>
    <code>project_created</code> and <code>project_settings_updated</code>
    record the console account as actor and the project acted on. A settings
    change lists the sections changed, never their values.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
